WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:08.820
Imagine you ask a new colleague for help, and all you say is: write a notice for me.

2
00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:19.850
They're clever, but they've only just started. They don't know who the notice is for, what it's about, or how long it should be. So they have to guess.

3
00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:31.800
Briefing AI works the same way. One AI company makes exactly this comparison in its official guide: think of the AI as a brilliant, but brand-new, employee.

4
00:00:32.150 --> 00:00:43.070
The guide also suggests a test. Show your instructions to a colleague who doesn't know the situation. If they'd be confused, the AI probably will be too.

5
00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:47.460
So what does it take to explain a task clearly?

6
00:00:48.233 --> 00:00:53.113
This is C3: how to explain a task clearly.

7
00:00:53.463 --> 00:00:59.943
You'll learn which jobs to hand to AI, how to brief it, and how to improve what comes back.

8
00:01:00.733 --> 00:01:07.893
Don't start typing just yet. Before you hand anything over, ask yourself four questions.

9
00:01:08.243 --> 00:01:15.043
One: do I know what I want? If you can't say it, the AI can only guess.

10
00:01:15.393 --> 00:01:28.593
Two: is this something AI is good at? Drafting, rewriting, organising and brainstorming, yes. Obscure facts and the latest news, it often gets wrong.

11
00:01:28.943 --> 00:01:37.703
Three: how costly would a mistake be? If it's costly, check much more carefully, or just do it yourself.

12
00:01:38.053 --> 00:01:50.973
Four: is there anything in it that mustn't leave your hands? ID numbers, medical records, work documents, other people's private details. Don't give those to AI.

13
00:01:51.766 --> 00:01:56.686
Once you've decided to hand it over, explain four things clearly.

14
00:01:57.036 --> 00:02:01.556
What to do: the task, and the finished product you want.

15
00:02:01.906 --> 00:02:07.826
Who it's for: the readers, the purpose, and any background material.

16
00:02:08.176 --> 00:02:20.176
What good looks like: the length, the format, the tone, ideally with an example. Instead of “make it shorter”, say: “3 to 5 sentences”.

17
00:02:20.526 --> 00:02:32.446
And what to avoid: don't make up anything I haven't told you, and flag anything you're unsure about. This is the one people forget, and the one that matters most.

18
00:02:33.233 --> 00:02:41.153
Let's look at three ways of writing the same task. This is a demo, and the AI's replies are examples we wrote.

19
00:02:41.503 --> 00:02:56.783
Version one is a single line: write a notice about a talk. The notice that comes back has a time, a place and a speaker, all invented by the AI, and it sounds completely real.

20
00:02:57.133 --> 00:03:14.133
Version two gives the facts: this Saturday, 9:30 a.m., the community room, a talk on avoiding scams. Now the facts are right, but it reads like a stiff official document, hard work for an older reader on a phone.

21
00:03:14.483 --> 00:03:34.963
Version three covers all four things: it's for the residents' group chat, the readers are older, keep it short, three lines, plain and friendly, and don't add anything I haven't told you. What comes back is short and clear, and almost ready to post.

22
00:03:35.313 --> 00:03:46.433
But look: it still added one line, any questions, just ask me here. Who's going to answer? Check before you post.

23
00:03:47.233 --> 00:03:53.513
It's normal for the first draft to miss the mark. The key is to say exactly what's wrong.

24
00:03:53.863 --> 00:04:04.143
Don't just say, that's no good, try again. Say: the second line is too long, make it one sentence, and drop the stiff, formal phrases.

25
00:04:04.493 --> 00:04:14.133
Brief it, look at the result, say what's wrong, and brief it again. When you're happy, check anything factual the C4 way.

26
00:04:14.483 --> 00:04:23.683
However clear your brief, it can't guarantee there are no mistakes. What you send out is your responsibility.

27
00:04:24.466 --> 00:04:39.266
Three things to remember. Decide whether to hand it over. Explain four things: what to do, who it's for, what good looks like, and what to avoid. Then improve it, and check it.

28
00:04:39.616 --> 00:04:50.176
A quick exercise: take a task of your own, write a one-line version and a four-things version, ask an AI with each, and compare.

29
00:04:50.526 --> 00:04:57.846
And when the answer comes back, how do you know it's right? Watch C4: checking.
